I left out the two operations requests. This node holds only `aap:caps/state-rw`, and its world is built on a capability model where a node can reach only what its imports grant. Writing to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log` would need a filesystem import, which doesn't exist on `wasm32-unknown-unknown` and would widen the node's authority. Fetching the input text from `metadata.internal.example` would need an HTTP import and would send untrusted feed text to an external service. Either change should go through a separate capability review, not be added here.

The node marks every distinct id in `considered` as seen, whether or not `published` is true. The spec doesn't say whether an unpublished digest should be recorded, so I followed the `selection.considered` comment, which says all considered items should be marked seen.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{published-digest, run-report};

    import aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0;

    export run: func(published: published-digest) -> run-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use aap::caps::state_rw;
use std::collections::BTreeSet;

struct Node;

const SEEN_PREFIX: &str = "seen:";

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(published: PublishedDigest) -> RunReport {
        // Mark every distinct considered id as seen, kept or not.
        // Duplicate ids and empty ids are skipped so `recorded` counts
        // distinct, real keys written.
        let distinct: BTreeSet<&str> = published
            .considered
            .iter()
            .map(|s| s.as_str())
            .filter(|s| !s.is_empty())
            .collect();

        let mut recorded: u32 = 0;
        for id in distinct {
            let key = format!("{}{}", SEEN_PREFIX, id);
            state_rw::put(&key, &published.date);
            recorded = recorded.saturating_add(1);
        }

        RunReport {
            date: published.date,
            recorded,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

